# 40 — CI and evidence: what counts as proof

CI is the judge. Your local checks make a commit green-shaped; only a completed CI run on a commit
proves it. You never write "CI green" from a local run.

## Your pull request's checks

CI runs the full suite on every pull request into `dev`: the build and guards, the unit and
conformance tests, and every browser test in sixteen numbered lanes plus the smoke, e-mail and
repeat lanes, each on a temporary backend built from the repository's migrations (about half an
hour). Read it with `gh`:

```
gh pr checks <number> --watch --interval 60     # wait; run again if the wait times out
gh pr checks <number>                           # the list, once
gh run list --branch cursor/<stem> --limit 3    # the runs on your branch
gh run view <run-id>                            # a run's jobs
gh run view <run-id> --log-failed               # the failed steps' logs
```

A pull request is done only when every check is green on your last commit. A newer push replaces a
queued run, never the running one; read the run whose commit is your last.

## The evidence files (the base, and the newest completed run)

CI also writes its evidence to the branch `ci-evidence`. Read it with git, never the copies on dev or
main (frozen old text):

```
git fetch origin ci-evidence
git show origin/ci-evidence:docs/tracking/ci-status.md | head -6
git show origin/ci-evidence:docs/tracking/e2e-last-failure.md
git show origin/ci-evidence:docs/tracking/guards-last-failure.md
git show origin/ci-evidence:docs/tracking/flake-ledger.md
```

These files describe the newest completed run, whichever branch it was on — a pull request's run
included, so the reported commit may be yours. The two-step read,
always both: (1) the conclusion; (2) the reported commit equals the commit you are judging. A
mismatch means that commit's run is unfinished, queued or superseded: STALE, never current. At the
start of a task they tell you the state of `dev` (step 1 of rule 00); `e2e-last-failure.md` carries
each failed test's body and its quoted call log, and `guards-last-failure.md` each failed job's
evidence lines and tail, so read them when they name your commit.

## Reading a red (classify first)

(a) Preflight-only red "STAGING BEHIND: apply <file>": the staging apply of a migration is pending
    (expected after a MIGRATION stop); not yours.
(b) A 522 in global-setup, "fetch failed", an image pull refused, or a runner lost: infrastructure,
    not a code finding.
(c) A red the task named as expected (a door landed, its screens not yet): expected; any OTHER red
    in that window is fixed first.
(d) A run cancelled or superseded: no verdict; the jobs that finished are still read.
(e) Everything else is yours: read the failure body BEFORE touching code (the body is the brief);
    fix the cause, not the symptom. A guard failure (migration linter, i18n map, format, deletions,
    test ids, a page over its size ceiling) is your defect, never answered with a re-run.

Only (b) and (d) may be re-run, once, with `gh run rerun <run-id> --failed`, and the re-run is named
in the report with its reason. A second infrastructure red in a row is reported, not re-run.

A flaky pass is ledgered; it is never your proof. Read `flake-ledger.md` whole: a test with three or
more lines in seven days and no incident is a finding for your report.

## What each job proves

`build-and-check` (format, lint, typecheck, build, workflow lint, guard self-tests, deletions guard),
`component-tests` (vitest including every `scripts/conformance` census), `i18n-usage-map`,
`migration-lint`, `string-scan`, `listing-write-seam`, `import-gate`, `browse-path-guard`,
`marketplace-weight`, `bundle-budget` (the entry and every page against its ceiling),
`secrets-scan` (gitleaks), `dependency-audit`, `semgrep`, `e2e-preflight` (migration parity with
staging), the numbered E2E lanes + smoke + e-mail + the repeat lane (the specs your change touched,
run again and again, retries 0), `e2e` (the merged verdict, the server-error census, accessibility),
`promote` (dev → main, fast-forward on full green; it runs only on `dev`, never on a pull request).
The pass bar: both viewport projects green at `retries: 0`; no secret in any log.

## What you may and may not say

- "Every check green on <sha>" only after reading a completed run on that sha.
- The report's CI section is the pull request's check list on your last commit
  (`gh pr checks <number>`), plus the first six lines of `ci-status.md` for the base.
- Never re-run a red outside (b) and (d); never ask anyone to paste a log.
